demy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045108/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "47d82ee7a93d18c924fc603304e9fa5f6a8f4baa04d8f7eb6d0b60c3e24462e5"} {"question" "q-353435" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045108/state.json" "file_sha256" "f7db60036502bb576f02b883fd885a93c3d25258f65013f5106a84183aa22e01"} {"question" "q-353435" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "400"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045108/state.json" "file_sha256" "f7db60036502bb576f02b883fd885a93c3d25258f65013f5106a84183aa22e01"} {"question" "q-118577" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $y=y(t),$ what is the carrying capacity of the following logistic growth model?\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=1\\,200y-3{y}^{2},\\,y(0)=50\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045108/state.json" "file_sha256" "f7db60036502bb576f02b883fd885a93c3d25258f65013f5106a84183aa22e01"} {"question" "q-118577" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=ky(a-y),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>y$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nA diagram showing a typical logistic growth curve is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045108/assets/57f4b604c546daed4f1cb3895191fae0ccac33b5c17aac705f203f0a837c7e69.png)\n\nFirst, let's factor the right-hand side of the given equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=3y(400-y),\\,y(0)=50.\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that $a=400.$ Therefore, the carrying capacity is $400.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045108/state.json" "file_sha256" "f7db60036502bb576f02b883fd885a93c3d25258f65013f5106a84183aa22e01"} {"question" "q-118577" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045108/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "47d82ee7a93d18c924fc603304e9fa5f6a8f4baa04d8f7eb6d0b60c3e24462e5"} {"question" "q-118577" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045108/state.json" "file_sha256" "f7db60036502bb576f02b883fd885a93c3d25258f65013f5106a84183aa22e01"} {"question" "q-118577" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "1"] ["math" "120"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "400"] ["math" "600"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045108/state.json" "file_sha256" "f7db60036502bb576f02b883fd885a93c3d25258f65013f5106a84183aa22e01"} {"question" "q-118577" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "400"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045108/state.json" "file_sha256" "f7db60036502bb576f02b883fd885a93c3d25258f65013f5106a84183aa22e01"} {"question" "q-118560" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The population $y(t)$ of mockingbirds in a forest follows the logistic growth model\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=0.6y-0.0003{y}^{2},\\,y(0)=250,\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is the time, in months, since the observations began. According to this model, what is the maximum possible population of mockingbirds?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045108/state.json" "file_sha256" "f7db60036502bb576f02b883fd885a93c3d25258f65013f5106a84183aa22e01"} {"question" "q-118560" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=ky(a-y),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>y$ is the carrying capacity. \n\nThe carrying capacity $a$ represents the maximum possible population. A diagram showing a typical logistic growth curve is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045108/assets/57f4b604c546daed4f1cb3895191fae0ccac33b5c17aac705f203f0a837c7e69.png)\n\nFirst, let's rewrite the right-hand side of the given equation so that it matches the form above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =0.6y-0.0003{y}^{2} \\\\  & =0.0003y(2\\,000-y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that $a=2\\,000.$ Hence, the carrying capacity is $2\\,000,$ and we conclude that the number of mockingbirds cannot exceed $2\\,000.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045108/state.json" "file_sha256" "f7db60036502bb576f02b883fd885a93c3d25258f65013f5106a84183aa22e01"} {"question" "q-118560" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045108/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "47d82ee7a93d18c924fc603304e9fa5f6a8f4baa04d8f7eb6d0b60c3e24462e5"} {"question" "q-118560" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045108/state.json" "file_sha256" "f7db60036502bb576f02b883fd885a93c3d25258f65013f5106a84183aa22e01"} {"question" "q-118560" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "250"] ["math" "2\\,000"] ["math" "2\\,250"] ["math" "450"] ["math" "600"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045108/state.json" "file_sha256" "f7db60036502bb576f02b883fd885a93c3d25258f65013f5106a84183aa22e01"} {"question" "q-118560" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2\\,000"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045108/state.json" "file_sha256" "f7db60036502bb576f02b883fd885a93c3d25258f65013f5106a84183aa22e01"} {"question" "q-118561" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The population $y(t)$ of sloths in a forest follows the logistic growth model\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=0.3y(2-\\frac{y}{300}),\\,y(0)=135,\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is the time, in years, since the observations began. According to this model, what is the maximum possible sloth population?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045108/state.json" "file_sha256" "f7db60036502bb576f02b883fd885a93c3d25258f65013f5106a84183aa22e01"} {"question" "q-118561" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=ky(a-y),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>y$ is the carrying capacity. \n\nThe carrying capacity $a$ represents the maximum possible population. A diagram showing a typical logistic growth curve is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045108/assets/57f4b604c546daed4f1cb3895191fae0ccac33b5c17aac705f203f0a837c7e69.png)\n\nFirst, let's rewrite the right-hand side of the given equation so that it matches the form above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =0.3y(2-\\frac{y}{300}) \\\\  & =0.3y(\\frac{600-y}{300}) \\\\  & =\\frac{0.3y}{300}(600-y) \\\\  & =0.001y(600-y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that $a=600.$ Hence, the carrying capacity is $600,$ and we conclude that the number of sloths cannot exceed $600.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045108/state.json" "file_sha256" "f7db60036502bb576f02b883fd885a93c3d25258f65013f5106a84183aa22e01"} {"question" "q-118561" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045108/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "47d82ee7a93d18c924fc603304e9fa5f6a8f4baa04d8f7eb6d0b60c3e24462e5"} {"question" "q-118561" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045108/state.json" "file_sha256" "f7db60036502bb576f02b883fd885a93c3d25258f65013f5106a84183aa22e01"} {"question" "q-118561" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "1\\,000"] ["math" "465"] ["math" "550"] ["math" "600"] ["math" "800"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045108/state.json" "file_sha256" "f7db60036502bb576f02b883fd885a93c3d25258f65013f5106a84183aa22e01"} {"question" "q-118561" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "600"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045108/state.json" "file_sha256" "f7db60036502bb576f02b883fd885a93c3d25258f65013f5106a84183aa22e01"} {"question" "q-353477" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The number $y(t)$ of people who have participated in a viral trend on social media follows the logistic growth model\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=1250y-5{y}^{2},\\,y(0)=40,\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is the time, in hours, since the observations began. According to this model, what is the maximum possible number of participants?\n\nThe maximum possible number of participants is\n{{field-1}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045108/state.json" "file_sha256" "f7db60036502bb576f02b883fd885a93c3d25258f65013f5106a84183aa22e01"} {"question" "q-353477" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=ky(a-y),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>y$ is the carrying capacity. \n\nThe carrying capacity $a$ represents the maximum possible number of participants in the trend. A diagram showing a typical logistic growth curve is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045108/assets/d8133f1c4203903bac606bbae61c73717e2ba2dc15a97a77f48eaaeaf4736d06.png)\n\nFirst, let's rewrite the right-hand side of the given equation so that it matches the form above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =1250y-5{y}^{2} \\\\  & =5y(250-y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that $a=250.$ Hence, the carrying capacity is $250,$ and we conclude that the maximum possible number of participants is $250.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045108/state.json" "file_sha256" "f7db60036502bb576f02b883fd885a93c3d25258f65013f5106a84183aa22e01"} {"question" "q-353477" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045108/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "47d82ee7a93d18c924fc603304e9fa5f6a8f4baa04d8f7eb6d0b60c3e24462e5"} {"question" "q-353477" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045108/state.json" "file_sha256" "f7db60036502bb576f02b883fd885a93c3d25258f65013f5106a84183aa22e01"} {"question" "q-353477" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "250"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045108/state.json" "file_sha256" "f7db60036502bb576f02b883fd885a93c3d25258f65013f5106a84183aa22e01"} {"question" "q-119849" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The population $y(t)$ of giraffes on a certain African wildlife reserve follows the logistic growth model\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=17.2y-0.004{y}^{2},\\,y(0)=180,\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is the time, in years, since the observations began. According to this model, what is the maximum possible population of giraffes?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045108/state.json" "file_sha256" "f7db60036502bb576f02b883fd885a93c3d25258f65013f5106a84183aa22e01"} {"question" "q-119849" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=ky(a-y),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>y$ is the carrying capacity. \n\nThe carrying capacity $a$ represents the maximum possible population. A diagram showing a typical logistic growth curve is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045108/assets/57f4b604c546daed4f1cb3895191fae0ccac33b5c17aac705f203f0a837c7e69.png)\n\nFirst, let's rewrite the right-hand side of the given equation so that it matches the form above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =17.2y-0.004{y}^{2} \\\\  & =0.004y(4\\,300-y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that $a=4\\,300.$ Hence, the carrying capacity is $4\\,300,$ and we conclude that the number of giraffes cannot exceed $4\\,300.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045108/state.json" "file_sha256" "f7db60036502bb576f02b883fd885a93c3d25258f65013f5106a84183aa22e01"} {"question" "q-119849" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045108/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "47d82ee7a93d18c924fc603304e9fa5f6a8f4baa04d8f7eb6d0b60c3e24462e5"} {"question" "q-119849" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045108/state.json" "file_sha256" "f7db60036502bb576f02b883fd885a93c3d25258f65013f5106a84183aa22e01"} {"question" "q-119849" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "180"] ["math" "1\\,720"] ["math" "400"] ["math" "4\\,000"] ["math" "4\\,300"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045108/state.json" "file_sha256" "f7db60036502bb576f02b883fd885a93c3d25258f65013f5106a84183aa22e01"} {"question" "q-119849" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4\\,300"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045108/state.json" "file_sha256" "f7db60036502bb576f02b883fd885a93c3d25258f65013f5106a84183aa22e01"} {"question" "q-119846" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The population $y(t)$ of dolphins in a certain ocean follows the logistic growth model\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=4y(7-\\frac{y}{200}),\\,y(0)=43,\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is the time, in years, since the observations began. According to this model, what is the maximum possible dolphin population?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045108/state.json" "file_sha256" "f7db60036502bb576f02b883fd885a93c3d25258f65013f5106a84183aa22e01"} {"question" "q-119846" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=ky(a-y),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>y$ is the carrying capacity. \n\nThe carrying capacity $a$ represents the maximum possible population. A diagram showing a typical logistic growth curve is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045108/assets/57f4b604c546daed4f1cb3895191fae0ccac33b5c17aac705f203f0a837c7e69.png)\n\nFirst, let's rewrite the right-hand side of the given equation so that it matches the form above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =4y(7-\\frac{y}{200}) \\\\  & =4y(\\frac{1\\,400-y}{200}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{200}y(1\\,400-y) \\\\  & =0.02y(1\\,400-y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that $a=1\\,400.$ Hence, the carrying capacity is $1\\,400,$ and we conclude that the number of dolphins cannot exceed $1\\,400.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045108/state.json" "file_sha256" "f7db60036502bb576f02b883fd885a93c3d25258f65013f5106a84183aa22e01"} {"question" "q-119846" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045108/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "47d82ee7a93d18c924fc603304e9fa5f6a8f4baa04d8f7eb6d0b60c3e24462e5"} {"question" "q-119846" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045108/state.json" "file_sha256" "f7db60036502bb576f02b883fd885a93c3d25258f65013f5106a84183aa22e01"} {"question" "q-119846" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "1\\,400"] ["math" "200"] ["math" "400"] ["math" "43"] ["math" "70"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045108/state.json" "file_sha256" "f7db60036502bb576f02b883fd885a93c3d25258f65013f5106a84183aa22e01"} {"question" "q-119846" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1\\,400"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045108/state.json" "file_sha256" "f7db60036502bb576f02b883fd885a93c3d25258f65013f5106a84183aa22e01"} {"question" "q-51379" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The population $P(t)$of rabbits in a particular forest follows the logistic growth model\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=4P-0.002{P}^{2},\\,P(0)=100,\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is the time, measured in years. For which value of $P$ is the rabbit population growing at the fastest possible rate?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045108/state.json" "file_sha256" "f7db60036502bb576f02b883fd885a93c3d25258f65013f5106a84183aa22e01"} {"question" "q-51379" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n{P}^{′}=kP(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>P$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nFor a logistic growth model, the fastest rate of growth occurs when $P=\\frac{1}{2}a.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045108/assets/9d8f5340bba4d3c407e43f2bc64a4aa2ea986be89b8024baaa4e8ae65e96e5d5.png)\n\nFirst, let's rewrite the right-hand side of the given equation so that it matches the form above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dP}{dt} & =4P-0.002{P}^{2} \\\\  & =0.002P(2\\,000-P)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that $a=2\\,000.$ Hence, the carrying capacity is $2\\,000,$ and $P$ grows at its fastest possible rate when \n\n$$\nP=\\frac{1}{2}a=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\,000=1\\,000.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045108/state.json" "file_sha256" "f7db60036502bb576f02b883fd885a93c3d25258f65013f5106a84183aa22e01"} {"question" "q-51379" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045108/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "47d82ee7a93d18c924fc603304e9fa5f6a8f4baa04d8f7eb6d0b60c3e24462e5"} {"question" "q-51379" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045108/state.json" "file_sha256" "f7db60036502bb576f02b883fd885a93c3d25258f65013f5106a84183aa22e01"} {"question" "q-51379" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "1\\,000"] ["math" "1\\,400"] ["math" "2\\,000"] ["math" "500"] ["math" "800"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045108/state.json" "file_sha256" "f7db60036502bb576f02b883fd885a93c3d25258f65013f5106a84183aa22e01"} {"question" "q-51379" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1\\,000"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045108/state.json" "file_sha256" "f7db60036502bb576f02b883fd885a93c3d25258f65013f5106a84183aa22e01"} {"question" "q-83974" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The population $P(t)$ of catfishes in a particular lake follows the logistic growth model\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.1P-0.002{P}^{2},\\,P(0)=5,\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is the time, measured in years. For which value of $P$ is the catfish population growing at the fastest possible rate?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045108/state.json" "file_sha256" "f7db60036502bb576f02b883fd885a93c3d25258f65013f5106a84183aa22e01"} {"question" "q-83974" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n{P}^{′}=kP(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>P$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nFor a logistic growth model, the fastest rate of growth occurs when $P=\\frac{1}{2}a.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045108/assets/9d8f5340bba4d3c407e43f2bc64a4aa2ea986be89b8024baaa4e8ae65e96e5d5.png)\n\nFirst, let's rewrite the right-hand side of the given equation so that it matches the form above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dP}{dt} & =0.1P-0.002{P}^{2} \\\\  & =0.002P(50-P)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that $a=50.$ Hence, the carrying capacity is $50,$ and $P$ grows at its fastest possible rate when \n\n$$\nP=\\frac{1}{2}a=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 50=25.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045108/state.json" "file_sha256" "f7db60036502bb576f02b883fd885a93c3d25258f65013f5106a84183aa22e01"} {"question" "q-83974" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045108/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "47d82ee7a93d18c924fc603304e9fa5f6a8f4baa04d8f7eb6d0b60c3e24462e5"} {"question" "q-83974" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045108/state.json" "file_sha256" "f7db60036502bb576f02b883fd885a93c3d25258f65013f5106a84183aa22e01"} {"question" "q-83974" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "100"] ["math" "25"] ["math" "250"] ["math" "5"] ["math" "50"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045108/state.json" "file_sha256" "f7db60036502bb576f02b883fd885a93c3d25258f65013f5106a84183aa22e01"} {"question" "q-83974" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "25"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045108/state.json" "file_sha256" "f7db60036502bb576f02b883fd885a93c3d25258f65013f5106a84183aa22e01"} {"question" "q-353487" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The number $P(t)$ of potential buyers in a market for a new product follows the logistic growth model\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=7200P-3{P}^{2},\\,P(0)=100,\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is the time, measured in years. For which value of $P$ is the number of potential buyers growing at the fastest possible rate?\n\n$P=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045108/state.json" "file_sha256" "f7db60036502bb576f02b883fd885a93c3d25258f65013f5106a84183aa22e01"} {"question" "q-353487" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n{P}^{′}=kP(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>P$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nFor a logistic growth model, the fastest rate of growth occurs when $P=\\frac{1}{2}a.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045108/assets/9019b26b417c757bbfab79afb39379c1fc7a892bc44e934c6f23c339deacfd68.png)\n\nFirst, let's rewrite the right-hand side of the given equation so that it matches the form above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dP}{dt} & =7200P-3{P}^{2} \\\\  & =3P(2400-P)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that $a=2400.$ Hence, the carrying capacity is $2400,$ and $P$ grows at its fastest possible rate when \n\n$$\nP=\\frac{1}{2}a=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2400=1200.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045108/state.json" "file_sha256" "f7db60036502bb576f02b883fd885a93c3d25258f65013f5106a84183aa22e01"} {"question" "q-353487" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045108/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "47d82ee7a93d18c924fc603304e9fa5f6a8f4baa04d8f7eb6d0b60c3e24462e5"} {"question" "q-353487" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045108/state.json" "file_sha256" "f7db60036502bb576f02b883fd885a93c3d25258f65013f5106a84183aa22e01"} {"question" "q-353487" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1200"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045108/state.json" "file_sha256" "f7db60036502bb576f02b883fd885a93c3d25258f65013f5106a84183aa22e01"} {"question" "q-119855" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The rate of growth in the number of turtles $P(t)$ that developed fungus on their shells in a certain aquarium follows the logistic growth model\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.6P-0.012{P}^{2},\\,P(0)=2.\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is the time, measured in months. For which value of $P$ is the number of turtles that developed fungus growing at the fastest possible rate?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045108/state.json" "file_sha256" "f7db60036502bb576f02b883fd885a93c3d25258f65013f5106a84183aa22e01"} {"question" "q-119855" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n{P}^{′}=kP(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>P$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nFor a logistic growth model, the fastest rate of growth occurs when $P=\\frac{1}{2}a.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045108/assets/9d8f5340bba4d3c407e43f2bc64a4aa2ea986be89b8024baaa4e8ae65e96e5d5.png)\n\nFirst, let's rewrite the right-hand side of the given equation so that it matches the form above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dP}{dt} & =0.6P-0.012{P}^{2} \\\\  & =0.012P(50-P)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that $a=50.$ Hence, the carrying capacity is $50,$ and $P$ grows at its fastest possible rate when \n\n$$\nP=\\frac{1}{2}a=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 50=25.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045108/state.json" "file_sha256" "f7db60036502bb576f02b883fd885a93c3d25258f65013f5106a84183aa22e01"} {"question" "q-119855" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045108/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "47d82ee7a93d18c924fc603304e9fa5f6a8f4baa04d8f7eb6d0b60c3e24462e5"} {"question" "q-119855" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045108/state.json" "file_sha256" "f7db60036502bb576f02b883fd885a93c3d25258f65013f5106a84183aa22e01"} {"question" "q-119855" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "100"] ["math" "12"] ["math" "25"] ["math" "50"] ["math" "6"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045108/state.json" "file_sha256" "f7db60036502bb576f02b883fd885a93c3d25258f65013f5106a84183aa22e01"} {"question" "q-119855" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "25"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045108/state.json" "file_sha256" "f7db60036502bb576f02b883fd885a93c3d25258f65013f5106a84183aa22e01"} {"question" "q-348210" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The population $P(t)$ of beavers in a certain river follows the logistic growth model\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.0008P(1\\,400-P),\\,P(0)=60.\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is the time, measured in years. For which value of $P$ is the beaver population growing at the fastest possible rate?\n\n$P=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045108/state.json" "file_sha256" "f7db60036502bb576f02b883fd885a93c3d25258f65013f5106a84183aa22e01"} {"question" "q-348210" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n{P}^{′}=kP(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>P$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nFor a logistic growth model, the fastest rate of growth occurs when $P=\\frac{1}{2}a.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045108/assets/9019b26b417c757bbfab79afb39379c1fc7a892bc44e934c6f23c339deacfd68.png)\n\nFrom our equation, we see that $a=1400.$ Hence, the carrying capacity is $1400,$ and $P$ grows at its fastest possible rate when \n\n$$\nP=\\frac{1}{2}a=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1400=700.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045108/state.json" "file_sha256" "f7db60036502bb576f02b883fd885a93c3d25258f65013f5106a84183aa22e01"} {"question" "q-348210" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045108/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "47d82ee7a93d18c924fc603304e9fa5f6a8f4baa04d8f7eb6d0b60c3e24462e5"} {"question" "q-348210" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045108/state.json" "file_sha256" "f7db60036502bb576f02b883fd885a93c3d25258f65013f5106a84183aa22e01"} {"question" "q-348210" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "700"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045108/state.json" "file_sha256" "f7db60036502bb576f02b883fd885a93c3d25258f65013f5106a84183aa22e01"} {"question" "e-9376" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following differential equation for the function $y = y(t)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\ny' = 2y\\left(60 - \\frac{y}{3} \\right), \\qquad y(0) = 10\n$$\n\nWhat is $\\displaystyle \\lim_{t\\to\\infty}{y(t)}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045108/example-9376.json" "file_sha256" "3b1fbc5434713a5d2dadf3740d5e6359c07086add74bc3008c1a711cc94e0f2b"} {"question" "e-9376" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\ny' = ky(a-y),\n$$\n\nwhere $k > 0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a > y$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nA diagram showing a typical logistic growth curve is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045108/assets/57f4b604c546daed4f1cb3895191fae0ccac33b5c17aac705f203f0a837c7e69.png)\n\nFirst, let's rewrite the right-hand side of the given equation so that it matches the form above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =2y(60-\\frac{y}{3}) \\\\  & =2y(\\frac{180-y}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2y}{3}(180-y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that $a = 180.$ Hence, the carrying capacity is $180.$\n\nFor a given logistic growth model, $y\\to a$ as $t\\to\\infty.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{t\\to\\infty}{y(t)} = 180.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045108/example-9376.json" "file_sha256" "3b1fbc5434713a5d2dadf3740d5e6359c07086add74bc3008c1a711cc94e0f2b"} {"question" "e-9375" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The population $y(t)$ of some foxes in a forest follows the logistic growth model\n\n$$\ny' =0.5y - 0.002y^2, \\qquad y(0) = 25,\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is the time, in months, since the observations began. According to this model, what is the maximum possible fox population?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045108/example-9375.json" "file_sha256" "fe6d8bdfc6b4e09f83f623b974aca3d7b3a657944b72ad7a703a2c4ce3d63e35"} {"question" "e-9375" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\ny' = ky(a-y),\n$$\n\nwhere $k > 0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a > y$ is the carrying capacity. \n\nThe carrying capacity $a$ represents the maximum possible population. A diagram showing a typical logistic growth curve is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045108/assets/57f4b604c546daed4f1cb3895191fae0ccac33b5c17aac705f203f0a837c7e69.png)\n\nFirst, let's rewrite the right-hand side of the given equation so that it matches the form above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =0.5y-0.002{y}^{2} \\\\  & =0.002y(250-y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that $a = 250.$ Hence, the carrying capacity is $250,$ and we conclude that the number of foxes cannot exceed $250.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045108/example-9375.json" "file_sha256" "fe6d8bdfc6b4e09f83f623b974aca3d7b3a657944b72ad7a703a2c4ce3d63e35"} {"question" "e-7324" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The rate of growth in the number of people $P(t)$ infected with a particular virus  follows the logistic growth model\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} P}{\\mathrm{d} t} =  4P - 0.000\\,01  P^2,  \\qquad P(0) = 5\\,000,\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is the time, in days, since the virus outbreak began. For which value of $P$ is the number of people infected with the virus growing at the fastest possible rate?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045108/example-7324.json" "file_sha256" "ffe5d8f5ea8111e859852f5f025f1ef8fbcf30c262862e0a7e9f2097a8c2f1f8"} {"question" "e-7324" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\nP' = kP(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $k > 0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a > P$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nFor a logistic growth model, the fastest rate of growth occurs when $P = \\dfrac12a.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045108/assets/9d8f5340bba4d3c407e43f2bc64a4aa2ea986be89b8024baaa4e8ae65e96e5d5.png)\n\nFirst, let's rewrite the right-hand side of the given equation so that it matches the form above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dP}{dt} & =4P-0.000\\,01{P}^{2} \\\\  & =0.000\\,01P(400\\,000-P)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that $a = 400\\,000.$ Hence, the carrying capacity is $400\\,000,$ and $P$ grows at its fastest possible rate when \n\n$$\nP = \\dfrac12a = \\dfrac12\\cdot 400\\,000 = 200\\,000.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045108/example-7324.json" "file_sha256" "ffe5d8f5ea8111e859852f5f025f1ef8fbcf30c262862e0a7e9f2097a8c2f1f8"}] "usage" {"e-7324" [] "e-9375" [] "e-9376" []} "basis" 1670 "retractions" [[17592186175799 :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation for the function $y = y(t)$:\n\n$y' = 2y\\left(60 - \\frac{y}{3} \\right), \\qquad y(0) = 10$\n\nWhat is $\\displaystyle \\lim_{t\\to\\infty}{y(t)}$?"] [17592186175799 :question/worked-solution "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\ny' = ky(a-y)\n$$\n\nwhere $k > 0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a > y$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nA diagram showing a typical logistic growth curve is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3551/Source/Images/0b183e2ca94cb514e6aec43fd4576cb7.png)\n\nFirst, let's rewrite the right-hand side of the given equation so that it matches the form above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{′} &= 2y(60 - \\frac{y}{3}) \\\\\n&= 2y(\\frac{180 - y}{3}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{2y}{3}(180 - y)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that $a = 180$. Hence, the carrying capacity is $180$.\n\nFor a given logistic growth model, $y\\to a$ as $t\\to\\infty$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{t\\to\\infty}{y(t)} = 180\n$$"] [17592186175798 :question/problem "The population $y(t)$ of some foxes in a forest follows the logistic growth model\n$y' =0.5y - 0.002y^2, \\qquad y(0) = 25$,\nwhere $t$ is the time, in months, since the observations began. According to this model, what is the maximum possible fox population?"] [17592186175798 :question/worked-solution "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\ny' = ky(a-y)\n$$\n\nwhere $k > 0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a > y$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nThe carrying capacity $a$ represents the maximum possible population. A diagram showing a typical logistic growth curve is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3551/Source/Images/23a52670fc85a225d9bd84a9110bd028.png)\n\nFirst, let's rewrite the right-hand side of the given equation so that it matches the form above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{′} &= 0.5y - 0.002y^{2} \\\\\n&= 0.002y(250 - y)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that $a = 250$. Hence, the carrying capacity is $250$, and we conclude that the number of foxes cannot exceed $250$."] [17592186175724 :question/problem "The rate of growth in the number of people $P(t)$ infected with a particular virus follows the logistic growth model\n\n$\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} P}{\\mathrm{d} t} = 4P - 0.000\\,01 P^2, \\qquad P(0) = 5\\,000$,\nwhere $t$ is the time, in days, since the virus outbreak began. For which value of $P$ is the number of people infected with the virus growing at the fastest possible rate?"] [17592186175724 :question/worked-solution "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\nP' = kP(a-P)\n$$\n\nwhere $k > 0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a > P$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nFor a logistic growth model, the fastest rate of growth occurs when\n\n$$\nP = \\dfrac12a\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3551/Source/Images/16751776874ece803120a91cd197180d.png)\n\nFirst, let's rewrite the right-hand side of the given equation so that it matches the form above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dP}{dt} &= 4P - 0.00001P^{2} \\\\\n&= 0.00001P(400000 - P)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that\n\n$$\na = 400\\,000\n$$\n\nHence, the carrying capacity is $400\\,000$, and $P$ grows at its fastest possible rate when\n\n$$\nP = \\dfrac12a = \\dfrac12\\cdot 400\\,000 = 200\\,000\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [] "immutable_fields" [] "no_history" []}
